# AGENTS.md

## Purpose

This repository uses a Codex-first, plan-first workflow. Durable repository
artifacts, not chat memory or provider scratch files, are the source of truth.

## Workflow Ownership

- OpenSpec owns behavior proposal, design, specs, tasks, verification, sync,
  and archive.
- DevFlow owns routing, readiness, evidence, review, release gates, and its
  control state below `.planning/devflow/`.
- The selected methodology profile implements optional engineering discipline.
- The selected roadmap provider alone owns root `.planning/` roadmap,
  milestone, phase, and phase-verification files.

Default selection is `core + none`: `workflow.methodology_profile: core` and
`workflow.roadmap_provider: none` in `.dev-flow.json`. External methodology or
roadmap providers are opt-in and project-local.

## Project Control Plane

- `AGENTS.md`: durable routing rules.
- `ENGINEERING_POLICY.md`: dependency, evidence, review, and release policy.
- `TASK_LEDGER.md`: Goal Contract, Provider Selection, task ownership, write
  sets, evidence, review gate, and status.
- `EVIDENCE_TEMPLATE.md`: command and TDD evidence shape.
- `REVIEW_CHECKLIST.md`: completion and archive review.
- `.planning/devflow/STATE.md`: DevFlow state; legacy root state is read-only
  migration input, never a DevFlow write target.

Provider drafts are inputs only. Promote approved content into OpenSpec, a
selected roadmap plan, or a DevFlow ledger before it satisfies a gate. Legacy
`docs/superpowers/specs/` and `docs/superpowers/plans/` follow this rule.

## Capability Routing

Route stable capabilities from `docs/provider_profiles.json`, not hard-coded
provider skills: decision resolution, implementation planning, test-first execution,
root-cause diagnosis, change review, completion proof, execution
orchestration, architecture guidance, goal definition, and roadmap lifecycle.
The configured profile supplies implementations. An unselected provider never
blocks readiness or contributes commands, links, hooks, or fallback behavior.

Provider selection is explicit config, then a matching lock, then unique
discovery. Ambiguous or stale sources block only that selected provider.
Activation, migration, lock persistence, and dependency changes require an
explicit apply gate. Provider-specific mapping and migration details live in
`docs/provider-profile-migration.md`.

## Intake and Planning

- Use `feature-intake` for a new feature, bug, refactor, migration, tooling, or
  workflow repair.
- Use `capability-research` when a solution depends on current or external
  capability, platform, plugin/runtime, API, hook, CLI, cache, or
  local-versus-platform evidence; the detailed evidence workflow lives in that skill.
- For research, design, architecture, product shape, or a technical plan,
  record a Skill Routing Ledger with request kind, workflow mode, required capabilities,
  OpenSpec/roadmap route, and reasons for skips. The ledger records
  `artifact-status: draft/final`,
  `capability-research: required/used/skipped`,
  `decision-resolution: required/used/skipped`,
  `decision-grilling: required/used/skipped`, plus
  `implementation-planning: required/used/skipped` and
  `architecture-guidance: required/used/skipped` explicitly.
- Open Questions make decision resolution required. Keep the artifact draft
  until evidence or one-question-at-a-time decision grilling resolves them.
- Use `ai-native-tech-plan` for a non-trivial technical or migration plan.
- Use `change-plan` to complete OpenSpec proposal, design, specs, and tasks.

Do not start implementation until scope, solution, validation, risks, and the
next ledger item are recorded.

## Goal Workflow

Route to `define-goal` when the user requests a goal or the work is
long-running, multi-slice, migration/release oriented, broad-refactor oriented,
cross-context, delegation-backed, or likely to lose its definition of done. A
goal contract names outcome, verification evidence, scope, non-goals, success
threshold, and stop conditions. DevFlow scripts may recommend goal mode but do
not call goal tools.

## AI Coding Planning Rules

Unless explicitly asked for a prototype or partial target, plan the complete
Target State. Do not use MVP, Future Work, calendar estimates, staffing, or
delivery phases to defer required behavior.

Every technical plan contains:

1. Target State and Scope / Non-Goals.
2. Architecture Decisions.
3. Completion Contract.
4. Dependency-ordered, production-complete Capability Slices.
5. Execution Ledger with owner, write set, evidence, and human gate.
6. Acceptance Criteria and exact Validation Commands.
7. Risks, rollback, review, and Final Verification.

Roadmap phases are sequencing containers, not technical completion boundaries.

## Repair Solution Discipline

For repair, after investigation, present the systemic and thorough solution first: root cause, affected
contracts, prevention, regression tests, docs, migration/compatibility, and
verification. Then justify systemic repair, minimal fix, staged repair, or deferred execution for
the current risk and approval boundary.

## Project Mode

Project mode: {{project_mode}}

For brownfield work, inspect architecture, conventions, tests, and specs first;
preserve compatibility and add characterization tests before risky changes.
For greenfield work, establish Target State, Completion Contract, OpenSpec, and
test/lint/build baselines before implementation.

## Workflow Mode Routing

- `Full OpenSpec` is mandatory for user-visible behavior, API, data model,
  persistence, integration, migration, permission, error handling, or
  compatibility changes. Configuration cannot bypass it.
- `Lightweight Ledger` is limited to configured docs-only, test-only, internal
  maintenance, or low-risk bugfix work and still requires scope, validation,
  execution log, and completion claim.
- `Prototype Mode` requires an explicit spike/prototype/demo request and records
  non-production status plus cleanup or promotion criteria.

Use `execute-task` for one approved item. Test-first execution is required for
features, bug fixes, and risky behavior changes. Do not expand scope or add a
dependency without updating the canonical plan and approval boundary.

## Delegation

Delegate only with authority, independent scope, and disjoint write sets. A
validated Agent Task Contract defines Goal, Scope, Constraints, Verification,
Evidence, and Human Gate. The main agent owns OpenSpec, `.planning/devflow/`,
shared docs, and integration unless writes are serialized. Review worker diffs
and rerun validation before updating the ledger.

## Verification and Archive

Before a completion claim, run fresh focused and broad checks, update the
Execution Ledger and `.planning/devflow/STATE.md`, record evidence, inspect the
diff, and report residual risks. Archive requires synchronized specs, complete
tasks, passing gates, explicit intent, and separate approval when risk remains.

## Plugin Eval Gate

When creating or updating Codex plugins or skills, resolve the release target
first and run `plugin-eval analyze` against the release counterpart. Use the dev
path only for diagnostics. default to fixing or optimizing findings before
completion. Deferral is an exception and records reason, residual risk and follow-up path;
record the score, findings, and optimization decisions.

## Local Reference Update Reminder

After major plugin or skill changes, update local Codex references and start with
`python3 dev/scripts/codex_auto_update_plugins_skills.py --json`. Report release
sync, installed plugin cache freshness, selected project-local skill links, and
migration conflicts. Apply requires explicit update intent.

## DevFlow Refresh Workflow

Use `dev-flow-refresh` to refresh the global DevFlow plugin before project-local configuration. Start
targeted with `codex plugin add dev-flow@cy-codex-skills --json`. Before project
apply, run migration diagnosis, workflow validation, cache-drift diagnosis,
scaffold dry-run, and `git status`. `AGENTS.md.generated` is merge-only; legacy
skill cleanup requires separate approval. Merge only durable workflow rules.

## Context Checkpoint and Compaction

At setup, design, plan, verification, archive, and shipped boundaries, persist
state, active OpenSpec/roadmap artifacts, changed files, command results, risks,
and next action. Store DevFlow checkpoints and compact results below
`.planning/devflow/`. Repository artifacts remain authoritative after compact.

## Forbidden Without Explicit Approval

- destructive cleanup or broad rewrite;
- public API or persistence-schema changes;
- production dependencies;
- bypassing failed tests;
- provider activation, migration apply, release sync apply, archive, commit,
  push, or PR creation.
